            "author_association": "MEMBER",
            "body": "Thanks for the suggestion!\r\n\r\nRight now all \"input\" surrounding (which find surrounding; as in \"delete\" and first step of \"replace\") is implemented via getting a single character surrounding identifier. Implementing cases like `sd2(` and `sr2)]` would require much complicated logic (taking into account that proper support needs to account not only single digit counts, but also more), which is not welcome.\r\n\r\nOther than that, most of use cases you describe can be done with dot-repeat:\r\n- `(foo (b*r)) baz` -> type `sd).` -> `*oo bar baz`.\r\n- `(foo (b*r)) baz` -> type `2sd)` ->`*oo (bar) baz`\r\n- `(foo (b*r)) baz` -> type `sr)].` -> `[*oo [bar]] baz`\r\n- `(foo (b*r)) baz` -> type `2sr)]` -> `[*oo (bar)] baz`\r\n\r\nSo `[count]` for this type of operations will remain to be the number *before* action mapping and will have [this meaning](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/79b1b3350f1cac7cef9e46a637f9adf2a14b4923/doc/mini-surround.txt#L390-L393).\r\n\r\nClosing as not planned due to bringing an overcomplication.",
            "created_at": "2024-01-31T10:49:56Z",
